<http://data.silknow.org/event/2c42fef1-f500-3bde-85af-9e8f048de869>
        a                              ecrm:E8_Acquisition ;
        rdfs:comment                   "Transferred from India Museum 1879. Slip book entry 558: 'Small piece of Fringe / Hyderabad, Deccan / 1855 / Presented by the Nizam of Hyderabad / Silver and Gold intermixed silk thread tie'"@en ;
        ecrm:P22_transferred_title_to  <http://data.silknow.org/actor/f0577f91-f887-3019-bf88-f9e5ba019390> ;
        ecrm:P24_transferred_title_of  <http://data.silknow.org/object/c0b1516f-137b-32df-b5f0-8fd66969140a> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "Transferred from India Museum 1879. Slip book entry 558: 'Small piece of Fringe / Hyderabad, Deccan / 1855 / Presented by the Nizam of Hyderabad / Silver and Gold intermixed silk thread tie'"@en .

<http://data.silknow.org/object/c0b1516f-137b-32df-b5f0-8fd66969140a/observation/3>
        a                   crmsci:S4_Observation ;
        ecrm:P2_has_type    <http://data.silknow.org/observation/general-observation> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note    "This decorative gold and silver fringe was made for embellishing dress or textiles. It was presented by the Nizam of Hyderabad to the Paris International Exhibition of 1855 as an example of the productions of the region. To make  tinsel, bars of silver or silver gilded with gold leaf were drawn through increasingly small  holes in iron plates in a process called wire-drawing. Once the desired thiness of the wire was achieved, it could be hammered flat to create long strips of tinsel. The tinsel could be used flat, or wrapped around a silk core to make metal-wrapped thread (zari).  This strip of fringe was made by plaiting tinsel into a band of green and silver-gilt-wrapped silk thread."@en ;
        crmsci:O8_observed  <http://data.silknow.org/object/c0b1516f-137b-32df-b5f0-8fd66969140a> .

<http://data.silknow.org/object/c0b1516f-137b-32df-b5f0-8fd66969140a/domain/1>
